{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01CHxD8JrUBjuNRenVH4CfBq","type":"tool_result","content":"28\tuse std::ffi::{c_char, c_int};\n29\t\n30\tunsafe extern \"C\" {\n31\t    // int printf(const char *restrict format, ...);\n32\t    #[link_name = \"printf\"]\n33\t    unsafe fn c_printf(format: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n34\t}\n35\t\n36\t/// Read a `char` parameter out of the raw argument register the way gcc does.\n37\t///\n38\t/// Both C entry points are declared `void f(char)`. On x86-64 SysV a narrow\n39\t/// integer argument occupies the low 8 bits of the argument register and the\n40\t/// upper 24 bits are *unspecified*; gcc consequently never trusts them and\n41\t/// emits an explicit `movsbl %dil, ...` (verified at `-O0`, `-O1`, `-O2`, `-O3`\n42\t/// and `-Os`), i.e. it truncates to the low byte and then sign-extends.\n43\t///\n44\t/// The exported wrappers below therefore take a `c_int` and truncate here,\n45\t/// rather than taking a `c_char`. Declaring the parameter as `c_char` makes\n46\t/// rustc attach LLVM's `signext i8` attribute, which *promises* that the caller\n47\t/// already sign-extended the value; with optimisations enabled LLVM then folds\n48\t/// `sext(trunc(edi))` down to a bare `edi` and the caller's upper bits leak into\n49\t/// `printf`. That diverges from the C for any caller that passes a wider value\n50\t/// through the same register (an unprototyped/K&R-style call, or an FFI binding\n51\t/// that declares the parameter as `int`), which is exactly what happens for a C\n52\t/// `enum` parameter handed a value with no valid variant. Taking the argument as\n53\t/// a full `c_int` and masking makes the low-byte-only behaviour explicit and\n54\t/// optimisation-independent, while remaining bit-for-bit compatible with every\n55\t/// conforming caller that passes a real `char`.\n56\t#[inline]\n57\tfn char_arg(raw: c_int) -> c_char {\n58\t    (raw as u32 & 0xff) as u8 as c_char\n59\t}\n60\t\n61\t/// `void printHexCharLine(char charHex)`\n62\t///\n63\t/// C body:\n64\t/// ```c\n65\t/// printf(\"%02x\\n\", charHex);\n66\t/// ```\n67\t///\n68\t/// `charHex` undergoes the default integer promotions to `int` before being\n69\t/// passed to `printf`. On this target `char` is signed, so negative values are\n70\t/// sign-extended and then printed by `%x` as an `unsigned int` (e.g. `-1`\n71\t/// prints as `ffffffff`). The C `printf` is used directly so that both the\n72\t/// formatted bytes and the stdio buffering behaviour match the C library\n73\t/// exactly.\n74\t///\n75\t/// `#[inline(never)]` keeps this a real, callable function body so that the\n76\t/// exported symbol is what `driver` actually invokes, mirroring gcc's\n77\t/// `jmp printHexCharLine@plt`.\n78\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n79\t#[allow(non_snake_case)]\n80\t#[inline(never)]\n81\tpub unsafe extern \"C\" fn printHexCharLine(charHex: c_int) {\n82\t    // Only the low 8 bits of the argument register are the `char`; gcc reads it\n83\t    // with `movsbl %dil, %esi`.\n84\t    let char_hex: c_char = char_arg(charHex);\n85\t    // Default argument promotion: char -> int (sign-extending on this target).\n86\t    let promoted: c_int = char_hex as c_int;\n87\t    unsafe {\n88\t        c_printf(c\"%02x\\n\".as_ptr(), promoted);\n89\t    }\n90\t}\n91\t\n92\t/// `void driver(char data)`\n93\t///\n94\t/// C body:\n95\t/// ```c\n96\t/// char result = data + 1;\n97\t/// printHexCharLine(result);\n98\t/// ```\n99\t///\n100\t/// `data + 1` is computed in `int` and then converted back to `char`, which\n101\t/// wraps modulo 256 (implementation-defined conversion as performed by gcc),\n102\t/// e.g. `127 + 1 == -128`. gcc emits `add $0x1,%edi; movsbl %dil,%edi`, so only\n103\t/// the low byte of the incoming register can affect the result — adding 1 to the\n104\t/// full register and then truncating is the same value as adding 1 to the\n105\t/// truncated byte, modulo 256.\n106\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n107\tpub unsafe extern \"C\"