{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_rx43qqt7xqfuzfeafarqk2gd4duwvnn45wxxo7yy5le5l5cpyxjq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Here's the summary.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md`: `nm -D` shows 0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols | ✅ symbol diff empty |\n| Phase B: every `CONFIGS.md` row passes across randomized inputs | ✅ 24/24 rows |\n| Phase C: every `ERRORS.md` row has a passing differential test | ✅ 13/13 rows |\n| Holds under every feature combination | ✅ 1 combo × 2 profiles × 5 C `-O` levels |\n\n**Build surface:** `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]`, and `CMakeLists.txt` has no options or conditional sources — so the only feature combination is the empty set. But `[profile.release]` *is* a real configuration axis, and it proved load-bearing.\n\n## Two real bugs found\n\n**1. Translation defect (fixed) — `char` argument not truncated to the low byte.** Caught by `ERRORS.md` row E9/E10, the out-of-range-value-across-FFI row. gcc compiles `void printHexCharLine(char)` to `movsbl %dil,%esi` — low byte only — at *every* `-O` level. The original Rust declared the parameter `c_char`, which makes rustc emit LLVM's `signext i8`, *promising* the caller sign-extended; with optimizations LLVM then folded the truncation away:\n\n```\nprintHexCharLine(int 0xdeadbe00)\n  C (ground truth):     \"00\\n\"\n  Rust debug:           \"00\\n\"        <- accidentally agreed\n  Rust release:         \"deadbe00\\n\"  <- divergent\n```\n\nFixed by taking the argument as `c_int` and masking explicitly. Release codegen is now instruction-for-instruction equivalent to gcc `-O2`. This was invisible in debug — the reason both profiles are now verified.\n\n**2. Harness hazards that caused vacuous passes.** Two, both caught by mutation testing rather than assumed away:\n- `cargo test` does **not** relink the cdylib, so tests were loading a **stale** `.so` — mutations \"passed\". Fixed with a staleness guard (`STALE Rust SHARED OBJECT`) plus `cargo build` in the driver script.\n- In-process `dup2` capture is contaminated by libtest's own progress text under default parallel execution (29–31/35 passing, varying per run). Fixed by capturing in a forked child; now 35/35 identically under `--test-threads=1` and full parallelism.\n\n## Suite validated by mutation testing\n\nRather than trust green checkmarks, I injected 11 mutations and rebuilt each in both profiles. **Every behavior-changing mutation was detected in both profiles**, including ones diverging at a single input out of 256 (`saturating_add`, `%02hhx`). The one undetected mutation was verified to be semantically identical code (`raw as u8` already truncates in Rust) — not a gap. Diagnostics pinpoint the offending input:\n\n```\ndiverges at output line 127 => input #127 = Some(127)\n  C line: ffffff80    Rust line: 7f\n```\n\n## Note recorded, not papered over\n\nThe upper 24 bits of a narrow argument register are ABI-*unspecified*, and C compilers genuinely disagree — a clang `-O2` build of the same C does **not** truncate. The translation matches **gcc**, which is what `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` actually builds with (`/usr/bin/cc` → GCC 11.5.0, no flags), i.e. the ground truth for this project. This is documented in `ERRORS.md` and `SYMBOLS.md` rather than left as a silent coincidence.\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified (md5sums verified identical; only the `build/` output dir was added). Deliverables: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `run_diff_tests.sh`, and 35 tests across `tests/{common,differential_valid,differential_errors,symbol_parity}`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1804,"cache_read_input_tokens":185391,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1804,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e645b772-5cf8-440b-be14-849c60bcd768","uuid":"d0d39412-e406-4f51-9595-2752f664da79","timestamp":"2026-08-18T01:11:18.089Z"}
